e8055ee4680bc9f6501382854b9984413eb6e0d2","rail":"lightning","sats":11,"at":1794018608,"comment":"Purchasing Bitbrand: Bitcoin Saved My Life by Bitcoin For The Arts via Fanfares.io","mint":"","sender":{"pubkey":"3e389987205b8ee825f9a788ffcdc99068a3c22a5c762b6f0066c82d37afcad5","name":"","picture":"","nip05":"","lud16":""},"recipient":{"pubkey":"a0e73529e04b4970a185e864b89e7d6e1ed148fdf08381b540ac020a742c62d5","name":"Bitcoin For The Arts","picture":"https://blossom.primal.net/9575f8c1dd218fbae6480943ab683c91a4445b5613145081182f0b467122ff3e.png","nip05":"art@bitcoinforthearts.org","lud16":"bfta@rizful.com"}}],"comments":[],"event":{"content":"Bitbrand makes hand-branded Bitcoin goods the old way: steel, fire, and leather. No ink, no printer, and no two pieces alike. He invented the Bitcoin branding iron, calls himself the branding iron broker, and built the company from the back of a van.\nIn this episode of Share Your Bitcoin Journey, Bitcoin For The Arts founder Dion Wilson meets Bitbrand in Jersey City, after they first met at the Midwest Bitcoin Summit in Columbus. Bitbrand tells how Bitcoin saved his life, how an ATM on the Upper East Side changed what he did with his money, and how he got sober at 30 and quit the corporate job. He open-sourced the branding iron. The Lake Satoshi crew bought one and branded their own coasters. A friend in North Jersey ships the orders and gets paid in bitcoin. Bitbrand keeps the books in bitcoin, not dollars, and puts the gains back into the work.\nHe is heading to El Salvador with a small run of handmade hats. He talks about paying for coffee through Square, why prices going up is a currency collapse, and what a $500 to $2,000 Bitcoin For The Arts micro-grant would unlock: materials, a sales rep, and better product.\nThis audio leaves out the Halloween skit. The full video is on YouTube: https://youtu.be/rXqC851gJSY\n21 sats unlocks this episode. They go to Bitcoin For The Arts.\nFIND BITBRAND:\nWebsite https://bitbrandshop.com